# The Endpaper - a bookbinder, a rebinding, and what is under the pastedown.
# Written for the library. One person, one trade, and the question of whether a repair is allowed to lose things.

title: The Endpaper
start: start

== start ==
bg workshop_bench
music room_tone
The bindery is one room over a shop that sells paper and envelopes and nothing else, and
you have had it for twenty-two years. Sewing frame, nipping press, a guillotine you will
not let anybody else use, and a shelf of glues that smell like a school.
People bring you things in carrier bags. That is most of the trade and it is not the part
you would keep if you could keep one part.
-> the_bag

== the_bag ==
The bag today has a book in it that was somebody's mother's, and the woman who brings it
says the words everybody says, which are that it is falling apart and she wants it done
properly and it does not have to look like it did, she just wants it to hold.
It is a Book of Common Prayer, 1901, and it is falling apart. The spine has gone, the
boards are detached, and the endpapers are foxed to the colour of strong tea.
-> the_quote

== the_quote ==
you: New spine, new boards under the old cloth, new endpapers. Three weeks. Twenty-eight
pounds.
woman: Do it.
She leaves and the bell goes behind her, and you put the book on the
bench and open it properly for the first time, which is the part of the job you have
never got tired of.
-> the_open

== the_open ==
The front endpaper is pasted down and it has been for a hundred and twenty years and it
has lifted at one corner, which is normal, and under the lifted corner there is writing.
Not a signature. A paragraph, in pencil, in a hand that is not the hand on the flyleaf,
and it goes under the pastedown and you cannot read more than four words of it.
The four words are: *I did not tell her.*
-> the_problem

== the_problem ==
The trade has an answer for this and the answer is that a pastedown is replaced with the
boards, because that is what rebinding means. Twenty-two years and you have replaced
hundreds of them and never once looked under one, because there was never anything to
look under.
The book is on the bench. The nipping press is behind you. She is coming back in three
weeks and she has paid a deposit of five pounds.
* Lift the pastedown. You have to know what it says. -> lift_it
* Ring her and ask what she wants done. -> ring_her
* Do the rebinding as quoted. The trade's answer is an answer. -> as_quoted
* Put it back in the bag and tell her you cannot do it. -> hand_it_back

== lift_it ==
You steam the pastedown off with a damp cloth and a spatula and it takes forty minutes
and it comes away in one piece, which is the good outcome and not the likely one.
Underneath, in pencil, on the board itself, is nine lines. It is a letter. It is dated
1943 and it is addressed to somebody who is not the woman's mother and it is not a
letter anybody ever sent.
-> the_letter

== the_letter ==
It is not a scandal. That is the thing. It is a man explaining, at length, and rather
badly, why he did not come back, and it is written on the inside of a prayer book where
it could not be sent and could not be thrown away, which is a place a person chooses.
You have it in your hand and it is not yours and it is not hers either, and the woman
coming back in three weeks is the granddaughter of one of the two people in it.
-> the_decision

== ring_her ==
You ring the number on the ticket and it rings out, and you ring it the next day and it
rings out again, and on the third day you write to the address on the ticket because
that is what the ticket is for.
The letter says what you have found in the terms of the trade, which are that the
pastedown is not original and there is writing on the board underneath it, and it asks
her to ring you.
She rings on the Thursday. She has read the letter four times.
-> the_ringing

== the_ringing ==
woman: Is it bad?
you: It's nine lines of a letter from 1943 and I've read four words of it.
woman: What four words?
you: I did not tell her.
There is a long pause on the line that is not a pause about the telephone.
woman: It's my nan's book. My nan's and my grandad's. He was in Italy in the war and he
came back in forty-six and they were married in forty-seven.
you: Then it isn't his writing, or it is and it is not to her.
woman: I know what it is.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
The book is on the bench with the pastedown half lifted and nine lines of somebody's
grandfather visible in pencil on the board, and the trade's answer is that the board goes
in the bin with the endpapers, because that is what rebinding is.
* Keep the board. Mount the nine lines inside the new cover. -> ending_kept
* Replace the board as quoted and say nothing more about it. -> ending_replaced
* Ring her and read her the nine lines down the telephone. -> ending_read_aloud

== as_quoted ==
You do the rebinding as quoted. New spine, new boards, new endpapers, the old cloth
lifted and laid back down, and it is a good piece of work and it takes the three weeks
it was going to take.
The old board goes in the bin with the old spine and the foxed endpapers and a hundred
and twenty years of somebody's mother's hands, and you do not read the rest of it, and
you will think about that for a long time.
-> END

== ending_kept ==
You keep the board. It is the wrong thing to do by the trade and it is not a hard thing to
do: you pare the old board down, lift the pastedown whole, and mount the nine lines inside
the new cover on a guard, where they are visible and where they are not on the outside of
anything.
She cries when she collects it and then apologises and then reads it twice standing at the
counter, and the second time she reads it out loud, which you had not expected and which is
the last thing you learn about the family in the book.
-> END

== ending_replaced ==
You replace the board as quoted and you finish the book to the three weeks and it is good
work and she is pleased with it.
The old board goes in the bin with the foxed endpapers and the detached spine, and you do
not take it out again, and you do not tell her, and there is nobody else alive who has read
any of the nine lines. You have decided that this is a kindness and you have decided it
more than once.
-> END

== ending_read_aloud ==
You ring her and you read her the nine lines down the telephone, badly, because they are
in pencil and they are 1943 and the man's hand goes to pieces in the last two.
She does not say anything for a while. Then she says thank you, and then she says the thing
that you have thought about since, which is that her nan kept it, and that keeping a thing
is not the same as not knowing about it, and that you should do whatever you think is
right with the board.
So you keep the board.
-> END

== hand_it_back ==
You put it back in the bag and you put the bag on the shelf under the counter and you
ring her and say you are not the right person for this one, which is true and is not the
whole of it.
She comes and gets it on the Saturday. She does not ask why. The book goes on being a
Book of Common Prayer with a detached board and a pastedown that has lifted at one
corner, and it will do that for another twenty years, and then it will do something else.
-> END
